    twentyFirstCenturySkills: ['Critical Thinking', 'Problem Solving', 'Communication', 'Collaboration'],
    background: `The Metropolitan Hotel is a 400-room landmark commercial property located in downtown Boston, Massachusetts. Situated adjacent to Copley Square, the hotel is an official host partner for the Boston Marathon, an international sporting event that attracts 30,000 runners and over 500,000 spectators annually. During marathon weekend, the property commands its highest room rates of the year, with standard king rooms booking at $520 per night and premium suites commanding upwards of $1,200, achieving 100% capacity months in advance.

To protect against cancellations and maximize yield, the hotel's revenue management department utilizes automated predictive algorithms. Historically, the property experiences an 8% "wash" rate—a combination of last-minute cancellations, missed flight connections, and travel no-shows. Relying on this historical statistical model, automated revenue controls authorized intentional overbooking up to 108% of total room capacity, selling 432 rooms against the hotel's 400 physical keys.

However, yesterday's weather across the Northeast was unseasonably clear, airline flight on-time performance was near 100%, and the enthusiasm surrounding the marathon resulted in unprecedented commitment from traveling runners. Only two cancellations occurred. Consequently, the property is currently oversold by 32 rooms with 100% of remaining incoming guests possessing confirmed, pre-paid reservations. Arriving guests include charity marathon runners who have trained for over a year, elite tier corporate accounts, and international travelers holding third-party online travel agency (OTA) vouchers.

By 2:00 PM, front desk lines stretched into the street. Two novice desk agents broke down in tears when an elite loyalty member threatened legal action after being informed their room was unavailable. The surrounding hotel market is at 99% occupancy, leaving competitor rooms virtually impossible to secure. The hotel's standard "walk policy" manual has not been updated in six years, leaving frontline staff with no pre-contracted partner rooms, no designated private lounge for displaced guests, and no clear authorization limits for cash or voucher compensation.

The General Manager of The Metropolitan Hotel (played by the judge) has convened an urgent executive council with you and your partner (Front Office Operations Manager and Reservations Lead). The General Manager demands an immediate, operational "Walk Management Protocol" to execute tonight. Your presentation must detail objective, fair criteria for determining which specific 32 guests are relocated, pre-arranged transportation and room coverage at comparable luxury properties, structured financial compensation packages, frontline de-escalation scripts, and long-term inventory forecasting adjustments to prevent catastrophic overbooking crises in the future.`,
